{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794526806452,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"id":"prt_1d22201b4001graWvlGe2tnWs5","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d221fbe70010zQ6dp594YUUkv","type":"step-start","snapshot":"ebe7c2dce9c1cd9640f82ae8bbf28b97e6e21ed4"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794526811872,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2221642001s3jloFa5a6mhHy","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d221fbe70010zQ6dp594YUUkv","type":"tool","id":"call_844fc07e696d424e98f80acc","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794526811714,"end":1794526811872}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794526811875,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22213ac001T5SnaHM0832fT2","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d221fbe70010zQ6dp594YUUkv","type":"tool","id":"call_53d84b3ed9554ba0aa4b072c","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs, lines 1-362\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: /**\n3:  * Check that a phrase a hub's router advertises actually reaches that hub.\n4:  *\n5:  * usage: ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs [--hub <id>] [--json] [--limit N]\n6:  *        [--concurrency N] [--skill <name-or-path>]\n7:  *\n8:  * A router's INTENT_SIGNALS resolves an intent inside a hub that has already been\n9:  * chosen. A hub's graph-metadata.json intent_signals is what decides which hub\n10:  * gets chosen. The two are not meant to match, so diffing them reports hundreds\n11:  * of differences that are not defects: a bare common word belongs in the first and\n12:  * would over-trigger in the second.\n13:  *\n14:  * The only thing that settles it is asking the advisor. This probes every\n15:  * multi-word phrase a router declares and reports the ones that do not reach the\n16:  * hub declaring them, which is the failure a diff cannot distinguish from design.\n17:  *\n18:  * Single words are skipped: they fail on length rather than on vocabulary, and\n19:  * reporting them buries the real cases.\n20:  */\n21: 'use strict';\n22: const fs = require('fs');\n23: const path = require('path');\n24: const { execFile } = require('child_process');\n25: const { promisify } = require('util');\n26: \n27: const execFileAsync = promisify(execFile);\n28: \n29: // Probing is the whole cost of this check, and it is spent waiting rather than\n30: // working: an uncached prompt takes about six seconds inside the advisor whichever\n31: // entry point asks, so the process spawn is noise. Sequentially that is over forty\n32: // minutes for the fleet, which is long enough that the check gets sampled instead\n33: // of run, and a sampled inventory is how phrases stayed broken across green runs.\n34: // The daemon serves concurrent callers, so the wait overlaps. The default is 2\n35: // rather than 8 because a busy advisor reported 11 to 13 probe-error rows at 8 and\n36: // none at 2, so concurrency was reading advisor load as unreachable phrases. The\n37: // measured price of the safer setting is about half a second per 48 cold probes.\n38: const DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY = 2;\n39: \n40: async function pool(items, limit, worker) {\n41:   const results = new Array(items.length);\n42:   let next = 0;\n43:   const runners = Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, async () => {\n44:     for (let i = next++; i < items.length; i = next++) {\n45:       results[i] = await worker(items[i], i);\n46:     }\n47:   });\n48:   await Promise.all(runners);\n49:   return results;\n50: }\n51: \n52: const REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(__dirname, '..', '..', '..', '..', '..');\n53: const SKILLS = path.join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled', 'skills');\n54: // Overridable so the fail-closed path can be exercised against an advisor that is\n55: // not there. A gate nobody can watch fail is a gate nobody knows the shape of.\n56: const ADVISOR = process.env.ROUTER_REACH_ADVISOR\n57:   || path.join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled', 'bin', 'skill-advisor.cjs');\n58: \n59: // Known ownership disputes the check may pass over. An entry pins the winner as\n60: // well as the phrase, so an allowed phrase that starts losing to somebody NEW still\n61: // fails: a blanket pass on the phrase would reintroduce the exact blindness this\n62: // check was written to remove.\n63: const ALLOWLIST_PATH = path.join(__dirname, 'router-reach-allowlist.json');\n64: \n65: function allowlist() {\n66:   if (!fs.existsSync(ALLOWLIST_PATH)) return new Map();\n67:   const raw = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(ALLOWLIST_PATH, 'utf8'));\n68:   return new Map((raw.entries || []).map((e) => [`${e.declaredBy}\\u0000${e.phrase}`, e]));\n69: }\n70: \n71: const ALLOWED = allowlist();\n72: \n73: function allowedFor(hub, phrase, winner) {\n74:   const e = ALLOWED.get(`${hub}\\u0000${phrase}`);\n75:   return e && e.wonBy === winner ? e : null;\n76: }\n77: \n78: // An operator can link private skills into the skills root under this prefix, and\n79: // the advisor scores one whenever its link resolves inside a trusted root\n80: // (system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/private-skill-target.cjs). The tracked tree\n81: // ignores every such entry, so CI never has one. This check gates the shipped\n82: // fleet: a verdict that flips with what one machine happens to link cannot be\n83: // fixed in the tracked tree, and the allowlist could not name the winner without\n84: // leaking a private name. A private surface that ranks ahead of the shipped\n85: // leader is still reported, as a note that never fails the run, because on that\n86: // machine it really does take the phrase.\n87: const PRIVATE_SKILL_PREFIX = 'local-';\n88: \n89: function isOperatorLocal(skillId) {\n90:   return typeof skillId === 'string' && skillId.startsWith(PRIVATE_SKILL_PREFIX);\n91: }\n92: \n93: // Ranks the shipped fleet out of one passing-recommendation list, and keeps the\n94: // confidence of every private surface that sat ahead of the shipped leader.\n95: function rankShippedFleet(above, hub) {\n96:   const shipped = above.filter((r) => !isOperatorLocal(r.skillId));\n97:   const leaderAt = shipped.length ? above.indexOf(shipped[0]) : above.length;\n98:   return {\n99:     hit: shipped.length > 0 && (shipped[0].skillId === hub || shipped[0].ownerSkillId === hub),\n100:     present: shipped.some((r) => r.skillId === hub || r.ownerSkillId === hub),\n101:     top: shipped.slice(0, 2),\n102:     localAhead: above.slice(0, leaderAt).filter((r) => isOperatorLocal(r.skillId)),\n103:   };\n104: }\n105: \n106: // Kinds reported for a human and never failed: a length limit, a pinned dispute,\n107: // and a private surface the tracked tree does not own.\n108: const NON_FAILING = new Set(['no-reach', 'allowed', 'operator-local']);\n109: \n110: function hubs() {\n111:   return fs.readdirSync(SKILLS, { withFileTypes: true })\n112:     .filter((e) => e.isDirectory())\n113:     .map((e) => e.name)\n114:     .filter((n) => fs.existsSync(path.join(SKILLS, n, 'mode-registry.json'))\n115:       && fs.existsSync(path.join(SKILLS, n, 'ROUTER.md')))\n116:     .sort();\n117: }\n118: \n119: function declaredPhrases(directory) {\n120:   const text = fs.readFileSync(path.join(directory, 'ROUTER.md'), 'utf8');\n121:   const block = /INTENT_SIGNALS\\s*=\\s*\\{([\\s\\S]*?)\\n\\}/.exec(text);\n122:   if (!block) return [];\n123:   const found = [...block[1].matchAll(/\"([^\"]{3,60})\"/g)].map((m) => m[1]);\n124:   return [...new Set(found.filter((k) => (\n125:     // a single token fails on length, not on vocabulary; reporting it buries the rest\n126:     k.includes(' ')\n127:     // a quote-delimited match is not always a phrase. Where a router formats its\n128:     // block across lines, the text between two quoted keywords is punctuation and\n129:     // structure, and probing it wastes a call and prints a row nobody can act on.\n130:     && /^[a-z0-9]/i.test(k)\n131:     && !/[{}[\\]]/.test(k)\n132:     && !/^(weight|keywords)$/i.test(k)\n133:   )))];\n134: }\n135: \n136: // A probe that could not run is not a phrase that reached nobody. Collapsing the\n137: // two lets a missing binary, a non-zero exit, a timeout or a cold daemon print a\n138: // clean pass over an advisor that never answered, so every failure to ask is\n139: // returned as its own result and fails the run.\n140: // The advisor exits 75 to say \"temporary, ask again\", and overlapping probes\n141: // provoke it: the same phrases that fail in a concurrent sweep answer correctly one\n142: // at a time. Reporting that as a defect would trade the old false green for a false\n143: // red, so a retryable exit is retried before it is believed. Anything else is a\n144: // real failure and is returned on the first try.\n145: const RETRYABLE_EXIT = 75;\n146: const PROBE_ATTEMPTS = 3;\n147: \n148: const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => { setTimeout(r, ms); });\n149: \n150: async function probeOnce(phrase) {\n151:   const { stdout } = await execFileAsync('node', [ADVISOR, 'advisor_recommend', '--json',\n152:     JSON.stringify({ prompt: phrase }), '--format', 'json'],\n153:   { encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 60000 });\n154:   return stdout;\n155: }\n156: \n157: async function reaches(phrase, hub) {\n158:   let raw;\n159:   let lastErr;\n160:   for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= PROBE_ATTEMPTS; attempt += 1) {\n161:     try {\n162:       raw = await probeOnce(phrase);\n163:       lastErr = null;\n164:       break;\n165:     } catch (err) {\n166:       lastErr = err;\n167:       if (err.code !== RETRYABLE_EXIT) break;\n168:       // Back off enough for the contention that caused it to clear.\n169:       await sleep(250 * attempt);\n170:     }\n171:   }\n172:   if (lastErr) {\n173:     const detail = lastErr.stderr ? String(lastErr.stderr).trim().split('\\n')[0] : '';\n174:     const retried = lastErr.code === RETRYABLE_EXIT ? ` after ${PROBE_ATTEMPTS} attempts` : '';\n175:     return { error: `probe failed: ${lastErr.code || lastErr.message}${retried}${detail ? ` (${detail})` : ''}` };\n176:   }\n177:   let envelope;\n178:   try { envelope = JSON.parse(raw); } catch {\n179:     return { error: 'probe returned unparseable JSON' };\n180:   }\n181:   const data = envelope && typeof envelope === 'object' ? envelope.data : null;\n182:   if (!data) return { error: 'probe returned no data envelope' };\n183:   const trust = data.trustState;\n184:   if (envelope.degraded || trust?.state !== 'live' || !Number.isInteger(trust.generation)) {\n185:     return { error: 'advisor response is degraded, not live, or lacks generation' };\n186:   }\n187:   const bar = data.effectiveThresholds ? data.effectiveThresholds.confidenceThreshold : 0.8;\n188:   const above = (data.recommendations || []).filter((r) => r.confidence >= bar);\n189:   const generation = trust.generation;\n190:   // Presence above the bar is not routing. A hub sitting second behind a higher\n191:   // scorer never answers the request, so the declaring hub has to rank first in\n192:   // the shipped fleet for the phrase to count as reaching it.\n193:   return { ...rankShippedFleet(above, hub), generation };\n194: }\n195: \n196: // A standalone skill declares its phrases in its own ROUTER.md and has no hub\n197: // registry to list, so the fleet scan cannot see it at all. Naming it directly is\n198: // the only way its reach can be reported, and the folder name is the id the\n199: // advisor's recommendation list carries.\n200: function skillTarget(value) {\n201:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.trim() === '') {\n202:     process.stderr.write('ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: --skill needs a skill name or path\\n');\n203:     process.exit(2);\n204:   }\n205:   const named = value.trim();\n206:   const directory = named.includes('/') || named.includes('\\\\')\n207:     ? path.resolve(named)\n208:     : path.join(SKILLS, named);\n209:   if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(directory, 'ROUTER.md'))) {\n210:     process.stderr.write(`ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: no ROUTER.md under ${directory}\\n`);\n211:     process.exit(2);\n212:   }\n213:   return { hub: path.basename(directory), directory };\n214: }\n215: \n216: async function main() {\n217:   const args = process.argv.slice(2);\n218:   // A misspelled flag must fail loudly rather than fall back to a default run.\n219:   // Only a token in option position is checked: the token after a value-taking\n220:   // option is its value even when it begins with a dash.\n221:   const valueOptions = new Set(['--hub', '--limit', '--concurrency', '--skill']);\n222:   const knownOptions = new Set(['--json', ...valueOptions]);\n223:   for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i += 1) {\n224:     if (valueOptions.has(args[i])) {\n225:       i += 1;\n226:       continue;\n227:     }\n228:     if (!knownOptions.has(args[i]) && args[i].startsWith('-')) {\n229:       process.stderr.write(`ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: unknown option: ${args[i]}\\n`);\n230:       process.exit(2);\n231:     }\n232:   }\n233:   const only = args.includes('--hub') ? args[args.indexOf('--hub') + 1] : null;\n234:   const skill = args.includes('--skill') ? args[args.indexOf('--skill') + 1] : null;\n235:   const limit = args.includes('--limit') ? Number(args[args.indexOf('--limit') + 1]) : Infinity;\n236:   const asJson = args.includes('--json');\n237: \n238:   // The fleet path answers \"which hub answers this\", the named path answers \"does\n239:   // this one root answer its own phrases\", so mixing them would print a row whose\n240:   // verdict nobody can read.\n241:   if (skill !== null && only) {\n242:     process.stderr.write('ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: --hub and --skill probe different sets: pass one of them\\n');\n243:     process.exit(2);\n244:   }\n245: \n246:   // A truncated inventory proves only what it sampled, which is how phrases stay\n247:   // broken across green runs. Sampling stays available by hand and is refused\n248:   // wherever the result is read as a gate.\n249:   if (limit !== Infinity && process.env.CI) {\n250:     process.stderr.write('--limit is refused under CI: a sampled run cannot gate a build\\n');\n251:     process.exit(2);\n252:   }\n253: \n254:   const concurrency = args.includes('--concurrency')\n255:     ? Math.max(1, Number(args[args.indexOf('--concurrency') + 1]) || DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY)\n256:     : DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY;\n257: \n258:   const report = [];\n259:   let generation = null;\n260:   const targets = skill === null\n261:     ? hubs().map((hub) => ({ hub, directory: path.join(SKILLS, hub) }))\n262:     : [skillTarget(skill)];\n263:   for (const { hub, directory } of targets) {\n264:     if (only && hub !== only) continue;\n265:     const phrases = declaredPhrases(directory).slice(0, limit);\n266:     const unreachable = [];\n267:     // Probes overlap, but the report is emitted in declaration order, so a run is\n268:     // diffable against the previous one.\n269:     const probed = await pool(phrases, concurrency, (phrase) => reaches(phrase, hub));\n270:     for (let i = 0; i < phrases.length; i += 1) {\n271:       const phrase = phrases[i];\n272:       const r = probed[i];\n273:       if (r.generation != null) generation = r.generation;\n274:       if (r.error) {\n275:         unreachable.push({ phrase, kind: 'probe-error', reaches: [r.error] });\n276:         continue;\n277:       }\n278:       // The private surface is never named: its folder name stays on the machine\n279:       // that links it, so the row carries only its confidence.\n280:       if (r.hit && r.localAhead.length) {\n281:         unreachable.push({\n282:           phrase,\n283:           kind: 'operator-local',\n284:           reaches: [\n285:             ...r.localAhead.map((t) => `operator-local=${t.confidence.toFixed(4)}`),\n286:             ...r.top.slice(0, 1).map((t) => `${t.skillId}=${t.confidence.toFixed(4)}`),\n287:           ],\n288:         });\n289:         continue;\n290:       }\n291:       if (!r.hit) {\n292:         const winner = r.top.length ? r.top[0].skillId : null;\n293:         const allowed = winner ? allowedFor(hub, phrase, winner) : null;\n294:         // Three failures wearing one symptom. Another hub ranked first while this\n295:         // one never surfaced is a routing defect. This hub surfaced but lost the\n296:         // top slot is the same defect wearing a passing score. Nothing above the\n297:         // bar at all is usually length, which vocabulary does not fix.\n298:         unreachable.push({\n299:           phrase,\n300:           kind: allowed ? 'allowed' : (r.top.length ? (r.present ? 'outranked' : 'wrong-hub') : 'no-reach'),\n301:           reaches: r.top.map((t) => `${t.skillId}=${t.confidence.toFixed(4)}`),\n302:         });\n303:       }\n304:     }\n305:     // A hub whose router parsed to nothing is a broken extractor reading as a\n306:     // clean hub, so an empty inventory is a failure rather than a quiet zero.\n307:     if (phrases.length === 0) {\n308:       unreachable.push({ phrase: '(inventory)', kind: 'probe-error', reaches: ['router declared no multi-word phrases'] });\n309:     }\n310:     report.push({ hub, declared: phrases.length, unreachable });\n311:   }\n312: \n313:   if (asJson) {\n314:     process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(report, null, 2)}\\n`);\n315:   } else {\n316:     for (const r of report) {\n317:       const errs = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'probe-error');\n318:       const wrong = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'wrong-hub');\n319:       const lost = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'outranked');\n320:       const none = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'no-reach');\n321:       const ok = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'allowed');\n322:       const local = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'operator-local');\n323:       const bad = errs.length + wrong.length + lost.length;\n324:       process.stdout.write(`${bad ? 'FAIL' : 'OK  '} ${r.hub.padEnd(28)} declared=${String(r.declared).padStart(3)} wrong-hub=${String(wrong.length).padStart(3)} outranked=${String(lost.length).padStart(3)} no-reach=${String(none.length).padStart(3)} allowed=${String(ok.length).padStart(3)} probe-error=${String(errs.length).padStart(3)} operator-local=${String(local.length).padStart(3)}\\n`);\n325:       for (const u of errs) {\n326:         process.stdout.write(`       probe-error ${u.phrase.padEnd(41)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')}\\n`);\n327:       }\n328:       for (const u of wrong) {\n329:         process.stdout.write(`       wrong-hub  ${u.phrase.padEnd(42)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')}\\n`);\n330:       }\n331:       for (const u of lost) {\n332:         process.stdout.write(`       outranked  ${u.phrase.padEnd(42)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')}\\n`);\n333:       }\n334:       for (const u of none) {\n335:         process.stdout.write(`       no-reach   ${u.phrase}\\n`);\n336:       }\n337:       for (const u of local) {\n338:         process.stdout.write(`       note       ${u.phrase.padEnd(42)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')} (ranks first on this machine only)\\n`);\n339:       }\n340:     }\n341:     const tally = (kind) => report.reduce((n, r) => n + r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === kind).length, 0);\n342:     const errs = tally('probe-error');\n343:     const wrong = tally('wrong-hub');\n344:     const lost = tally('outranked');\n345:     const none = tally('no-reach');\n346:     const allowed = tally('allowed');\n347:     const local = tally('operator-local');\n348:     process.stdout.write(`\\nchecked=${report.length} hub(s), wrong-hub=${wrong}, outranked=${lost}, no-reach=${none}, allowed=${allowed}, probe-error=${errs}, operator-local=${local}\\n`);\n349:     process.stdout.write(`advisor generation: ${generation == null ? 'unknown' : generation}\\n`);\n350:     // A phrase that reaches nobody is reported rather than failed: it is usually a\n351:     // length limit, and failing on it would make the check unrunnable. Everything\n352:     // else is a real defect, and a probe that never ran is the worst of them,\n353:     // because it is the one that would otherwise print a pass.\n354:     process.stdout.write(errs + wrong + lost ? 'RESULT: FAILED\\n' : 'RESULT: PASSED\\n');\n355:   }\n356:   const failed = report.some((r) => r.unreachable.some((u) => !NON_FAILING.has(u.kind)));\n357:   process.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);\n358: }\n359: \n360: if (require.main === module) main();\n361: \n362: module.exports = { DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY };","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs, lines 1-362\n1: #!/usr/bin/env node\n2: /**\n3:  * Check that a phrase a hub's router advertises actually reaches that hub.\n4:  *\n5:  * usage: ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs [--hub <id>] [--json] [--limit N]\n6:  *        [--concurrency N] [--skill <name-or-path>]\n7:  *\n8:  * A router's INTENT_SIGNALS resolves an intent inside a hub that has already been\n9:  * chosen. A hub's graph-metadata.json intent_signals is what decides which hub\n10:  * gets chosen. The two are not meant to match, so diffing them reports hundreds\n11:  * of differences that are not defects: a bare common word belongs in the first and\n12:  * would over-trigger in the second.\n13:  *\n14:  * The only thing that settles it is asking the advisor. This probes every\n15:  * multi-word phrase a router declares and reports the ones that do not reach the\n16:  * hub declaring them, which is the failure a diff cannot distinguish from design.\n17:  *\n18:  * Single words are skipped: they fail on length rather than on vocabulary, and\n19:  * reporting them buries the real cases.\n20:  */\n21: 'use strict';\n22: const fs = require('fs');\n23: const path = require('path');\n24: const { execFile } = require('child_process');\n25: const { promisify } = require('util');\n26: \n27: const execFileAsync = promisify(execFile);\n28: \n29: // Probing is the whole cost of this check, and it is spent waiting rather than\n30: // working: an uncached prompt takes about six seconds inside the advisor whichever\n31: // entry point asks, so the process spawn is noise. Sequentially that is over forty\n32: // minutes for the fleet, which is long enough that the check gets sampled instead\n33: // of run, and a sampled inventory is how phrases stayed broken across green runs.\n34: // The daemon serves concurrent callers, so the wait overlaps. The default is 2\n35: // rather than 8 because a busy advisor reported 11 to 13 probe-error rows at 8 and\n36: // none at 2, so concurrency was reading advisor load as unreachable phrases. The\n37: // measured price of the safer setting is about half a second per 48 cold probes.\n38: const DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY = 2;\n39: \n40: async function pool(items, limit, worker) {\n41:   const results = new Array(items.length);\n42:   let next = 0;\n43:   const runners = Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, async () => {\n44:     for (let i = next++; i < items.length; i = next++) {\n45:       results[i] = await worker(items[i], i);\n46:     }\n47:   });\n48:   await Promise.all(runners);\n49:   return results;\n50: }\n51: \n52: const REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(__dirname, '..', '..', '..', '..', '..');\n53: const SKILLS = path.join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled', 'skills');\n54: // Overridable so the fail-closed path can be exercised against an advisor that is\n55: // not there. A gate nobody can watch fail is a gate nobody knows the shape of.\n56: const ADVISOR = process.env.ROUTER_REACH_ADVISOR\n57:   || path.join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled', 'bin', 'skill-advisor.cjs');\n58: \n59: // Known ownership disputes the check may pass over. An entry pins the winner as\n60: // well as the phrase, so an allowed phrase that starts losing to somebody NEW still\n61: // fails: a blanket pass on the phrase would reintroduce the exact blindness this\n62: // check was written to remove.\n63: const ALLOWLIST_PATH = path.join(__dirname, 'router-reach-allowlist.json');\n64: \n65: function allowlist() {\n66:   if (!fs.existsSync(ALLOWLIST_PATH)) return new Map();\n67:   const raw = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(ALLOWLIST_PATH, 'utf8'));\n68:   return new Map((raw.entries || []).map((e) => [`${e.declaredBy}\\u0000${e.phrase}`, e]));\n69: }\n70: \n71: const ALLOWED = allowlist();\n72: \n73: function allowedFor(hub, phrase, winner) {\n74:   const e = ALLOWED.get(`${hub}\\u0000${phrase}`);\n75:   return e && e.wonBy === winner ? e : null;\n76: }\n77: \n78: // An operator can link private skills into the skills root under this prefix, and\n79: // the advisor scores one whenever its link resolves inside a trusted root\n80: // (system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/private-skill-target.cjs). The tracked tree\n81: // ignores every such entry, so CI never has one. This check gates the shipped\n82: // fleet: a verdict that flips with what one machine happens to link cannot be\n83: // fixed in the tracked tree, and the allowlist could not name the winner without\n84: // leaking a private name. A private surface that ranks ahead of the shipped\n85: // leader is still reported, as a note that never fails the run, because on that\n86: // machine it really does take the phrase.\n87: const PRIVATE_SKILL_PREFIX = 'local-';\n88: \n89: function isOperatorLocal(skillId) {\n90:   return typeof skillId === 'string' && skillId.startsWith(PRIVATE_SKILL_PREFIX);\n91: }\n92: \n93: // Ranks the shipped fleet out of one passing-recommendation list, and keeps the\n94: // confidence of every private surface that sat ahead of the shipped leader.\n95: function rankShippedFleet(above, hub) {\n96:   const shipped = above.filter((r) => !isOperatorLocal(r.skillId));\n97:   const leaderAt = shipped.length ? above.indexOf(shipped[0]) : above.length;\n98:   return {\n99:     hit: shipped.length > 0 && (shipped[0].skillId === hub || shipped[0].ownerSkillId === hub),\n100:     present: shipped.some((r) => r.skillId === hub || r.ownerSkillId === hub),\n101:     top: shipped.slice(0, 2),\n102:     localAhead: above.slice(0, leaderAt).filter((r) => isOperatorLocal(r.skillId)),\n103:   };\n104: }\n105: \n106: // Kinds reported for a human and never failed: a length limit, a pinned dispute,\n107: // and a private surface the tracked tree does not own.\n108: const NON_FAILING = new Set(['no-reach', 'allowed', 'operator-local']);\n109: \n110: function hubs() {\n111:   return fs.readdirSync(SKILLS, { withFileTypes: true })\n112:     .filter((e) => e.isDirectory())\n113:     .map((e) => e.name)\n114:     .filter((n) => fs.existsSync(path.join(SKILLS, n, 'mode-registry.json'))\n115:       && fs.existsSync(path.join(SKILLS, n, 'ROUTER.md')))\n116:     .sort();\n117: }\n118: \n119: function declaredPhrases(directory) {\n120:   const text = fs.readFileSync(path.join(directory, 'ROUTER.md'), 'utf8');\n121:   const block = /INTENT_SIGNALS\\s*=\\s*\\{([\\s\\S]*?)\\n\\}/.exec(text);\n122:   if (!block) return [];\n123:   const found = [...block[1].matchAll(/\"([^\"]{3,60})\"/g)].map((m) => m[1]);\n124:   return [...new Set(found.filter((k) => (\n125:     // a single token fails on length, not on vocabulary; reporting it buries the rest\n126:     k.includes(' ')\n127:     // a quote-delimited match is not always a phrase. Where a router formats its\n128:     // block across lines, the text between two quoted keywords is punctuation and\n129:     // structure, and probing it wastes a call and prints a row nobody can act on.\n130:     && /^[a-z0-9]/i.test(k)\n131:     && !/[{}[\\]]/.test(k)\n132:     && !/^(weight|keywords)$/i.test(k)\n133:   )))];\n134: }\n135: \n136: // A probe that could not run is not a phrase that reached nobody. Collapsing the\n137: // two lets a missing binary, a non-zero exit, a timeout or a cold daemon print a\n138: // clean pass over an advisor that never answered, so every failure to ask is\n139: // returned as its own result and fails the run.\n140: // The advisor exits 75 to say \"temporary, ask again\", and overlapping probes\n141: // provoke it: the same phrases that fail in a concurrent sweep answer correctly one\n142: // at a time. Reporting that as a defect would trade the old false green for a false\n143: // red, so a retryable exit is retried before it is believed. Anything else is a\n144: // real failure and is returned on the first try.\n145: const RETRYABLE_EXIT = 75;\n146: const PROBE_ATTEMPTS = 3;\n147: \n148: const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => { setTimeout(r, ms); });\n149: \n150: async function probeOnce(phrase) {\n151:   const { stdout } = await execFileAsync('node', [ADVISOR, 'advisor_recommend', '--json',\n152:     JSON.stringify({ prompt: phrase }), '--format', 'json'],\n153:   { encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 60000 });\n154:   return stdout;\n155: }\n156: \n157: async function reaches(phrase, hub) {\n158:   let raw;\n159:   let lastErr;\n160:   for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= PROBE_ATTEMPTS; attempt += 1) {\n161:     try {\n162:       raw = await probeOnce(phrase);\n163:       lastErr = null;\n164:       break;\n165:     } catch (err) {\n166:       lastErr = err;\n167:       if (err.code !== RETRYABLE_EXIT) break;\n168:       // Back off enough for the contention that caused it to clear.\n169:       await sleep(250 * attempt);\n170:     }\n171:   }\n172:   if (lastErr) {\n173:     const detail = lastErr.stderr ? String(lastErr.stderr).trim().split('\\n')[0] : '';\n174:     const retried = lastErr.code === RETRYABLE_EXIT ? ` after ${PROBE_ATTEMPTS} attempts` : '';\n175:     return { error: `probe failed: ${lastErr.code || lastErr.message}${retried}${detail ? ` (${detail})` : ''}` };\n176:   }\n177:   let envelope;\n178:   try { envelope = JSON.parse(raw); } catch {\n179:     return { error: 'probe returned unparseable JSON' };\n180:   }\n181:   const data = envelope && typeof envelope === 'object' ? envelope.data : null;\n182:   if (!data) return { error: 'probe returned no data envelope' };\n183:   const trust = data.trustState;\n184:   if (envelope.degraded || trust?.state !== 'live' || !Number.isInteger(trust.generation)) {\n185:     return { error: 'advisor response is degraded, not live, or lacks generation' };\n186:   }\n187:   const bar = data.effectiveThresholds ? data.effectiveThresholds.confidenceThreshold : 0.8;\n188:   const above = (data.recommendations || []).filter((r) => r.confidence >= bar);\n189:   const generation = trust.generation;\n190:   // Presence above the bar is not routing. A hub sitting second behind a higher\n191:   // scorer never answers the request, so the declaring hub has to rank first in\n192:   // the shipped fleet for the phrase to count as reaching it.\n193:   return { ...rankShippedFleet(above, hub), generation };\n194: }\n195: \n196: // A standalone skill declares its phrases in its own ROUTER.md and has no hub\n197: // registry to list, so the fleet scan cannot see it at all. Naming it directly is\n198: // the only way its reach can be reported, and the folder name is the id the\n199: // advisor's recommendation list carries.\n200: function skillTarget(value) {\n201:   if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.trim() === '') {\n202:     process.stderr.write('ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: --skill needs a skill name or path\\n');\n203:     process.exit(2);\n204:   }\n205:   const named = value.trim();\n206:   const directory = named.includes('/') || named.includes('\\\\')\n207:     ? path.resolve(named)\n208:     : path.join(SKILLS, named);\n209:   if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(directory, 'ROUTER.md'))) {\n210:     process.stderr.write(`ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: no ROUTER.md under ${directory}\\n`);\n211:     process.exit(2);\n212:   }\n213:   return { hub: path.basename(directory), directory };\n214: }\n215: \n216: async function main() {\n217:   const args = process.argv.slice(2);\n218:   // A misspelled flag must fail loudly rather than fall back to a default run.\n219:   // Only a token in option position is checked: the token after a value-taking\n220:   // option is its value even when it begins with a dash.\n221:   const valueOptions = new Set(['--hub', '--limit', '--concurrency', '--skill']);\n222:   const knownOptions = new Set(['--json', ...valueOptions]);\n223:   for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i += 1) {\n224:     if (valueOptions.has(args[i])) {\n225:       i += 1;\n226:       continue;\n227:     }\n228:     if (!knownOptions.has(args[i]) && args[i].startsWith('-')) {\n229:       process.stderr.write(`ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: unknown option: ${args[i]}\\n`);\n230:       process.exit(2);\n231:     }\n232:   }\n233:   const only = args.includes('--hub') ? args[args.indexOf('--hub') + 1] : null;\n234:   const skill = args.includes('--skill') ? args[args.indexOf('--skill') + 1] : null;\n235:   const limit = args.includes('--limit') ? Number(args[args.indexOf('--limit') + 1]) : Infinity;\n236:   const asJson = args.includes('--json');\n237: \n238:   // The fleet path answers \"which hub answers this\", the named path answers \"does\n239:   // this one root answer its own phrases\", so mixing them would print a row whose\n240:   // verdict nobody can read.\n241:   if (skill !== null && only) {\n242:     process.stderr.write('ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs: --hub and --skill probe different sets: pass one of them\\n');\n243:     process.exit(2);\n244:   }\n245: \n246:   // A truncated inventory proves only what it sampled, which is how phrases stay\n247:   // broken across green runs. Sampling stays available by hand and is refused\n248:   // wherever the result is read as a gate.\n249:   if (limit !== Infinity && process.env.CI) {\n250:     process.stderr.write('--limit is refused under CI: a sampled run cannot gate a build\\n');\n251:     process.exit(2);\n252:   }\n253: \n254:   const concurrency = args.includes('--concurrency')\n255:     ? Math.max(1, Number(args[args.indexOf('--concurrency') + 1]) || DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY)\n256:     : DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY;\n257: \n258:   const report = [];\n259:   let generation = null;\n260:   const targets = skill === null\n261:     ? hubs().map((hub) => ({ hub, directory: path.join(SKILLS, hub) }))\n262:     : [skillTarget(skill)];\n263:   for (const { hub, directory } of targets) {\n264:     if (only && hub !== only) continue;\n265:     const phrases = declaredPhrases(directory).slice(0, limit);\n266:     const unreachable = [];\n267:     // Probes overlap, but the report is emitted in declaration order, so a run is\n268:     // diffable against the previous one.\n269:     const probed = await pool(phrases, concurrency, (phrase) => reaches(phrase, hub));\n270:     for (let i = 0; i < phrases.length; i += 1) {\n271:       const phrase = phrases[i];\n272:       const r = probed[i];\n273:       if (r.generation != null) generation = r.generation;\n274:       if (r.error) {\n275:         unreachable.push({ phrase, kind: 'probe-error', reaches: [r.error] });\n276:         continue;\n277:       }\n278:       // The private surface is never named: its folder name stays on the machine\n279:       // that links it, so the row carries only its confidence.\n280:       if (r.hit && r.localAhead.length) {\n281:         unreachable.push({\n282:           phrase,\n283:           kind: 'operator-local',\n284:           reaches: [\n285:             ...r.localAhead.map((t) => `operator-local=${t.confidence.toFixed(4)}`),\n286:             ...r.top.slice(0, 1).map((t) => `${t.skillId}=${t.confidence.toFixed(4)}`),\n287:           ],\n288:         });\n289:         continue;\n290:       }\n291:       if (!r.hit) {\n292:         const winner = r.top.length ? r.top[0].skillId : null;\n293:         const allowed = winner ? allowedFor(hub, phrase, winner) : null;\n294:         // Three failures wearing one symptom. Another hub ranked first while this\n295:         // one never surfaced is a routing defect. This hub surfaced but lost the\n296:         // top slot is the same defect wearing a passing score. Nothing above the\n297:         // bar at all is usually length, which vocabulary does not fix.\n298:         unreachable.push({\n299:           phrase,\n300:           kind: allowed ? 'allowed' : (r.top.length ? (r.present ? 'outranked' : 'wrong-hub') : 'no-reach'),\n301:           reaches: r.top.map((t) => `${t.skillId}=${t.confidence.toFixed(4)}`),\n302:         });\n303:       }\n304:     }\n305:     // A hub whose router parsed to nothing is a broken extractor reading as a\n306:     // clean hub, so an empty inventory is a failure rather than a quiet zero.\n307:     if (phrases.length === 0) {\n308:       unreachable.push({ phrase: '(inventory)', kind: 'probe-error', reaches: ['router declared no multi-word phrases'] });\n309:     }\n310:     report.push({ hub, declared: phrases.length, unreachable });\n311:   }\n312: \n313:   if (asJson) {\n314:     process.stdout.write(`${JSON.stringify(report, null, 2)}\\n`);\n315:   } else {\n316:     for (const r of report) {\n317:       const errs = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'probe-error');\n318:       const wrong = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'wrong-hub');\n319:       const lost = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'outranked');\n320:       const none = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'no-reach');\n321:       const ok = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'allowed');\n322:       const local = r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === 'operator-local');\n323:       const bad = errs.length + wrong.length + lost.length;\n324:       process.stdout.write(`${bad ? 'FAIL' : 'OK  '} ${r.hub.padEnd(28)} declared=${String(r.declared).padStart(3)} wrong-hub=${String(wrong.length).padStart(3)} outranked=${String(lost.length).padStart(3)} no-reach=${String(none.length).padStart(3)} allowed=${String(ok.length).padStart(3)} probe-error=${String(errs.length).padStart(3)} operator-local=${String(local.length).padStart(3)}\\n`);\n325:       for (const u of errs) {\n326:         process.stdout.write(`       probe-error ${u.phrase.padEnd(41)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')}\\n`);\n327:       }\n328:       for (const u of wrong) {\n329:         process.stdout.write(`       wrong-hub  ${u.phrase.padEnd(42)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')}\\n`);\n330:       }\n331:       for (const u of lost) {\n332:         process.stdout.write(`       outranked  ${u.phrase.padEnd(42)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')}\\n`);\n333:       }\n334:       for (const u of none) {\n335:         process.stdout.write(`       no-reach   ${u.phrase}\\n`);\n336:       }\n337:       for (const u of local) {\n338:         process.stdout.write(`       note       ${u.phrase.padEnd(42)} ${u.reaches.join(', ')} (ranks first on this machine only)\\n`);\n339:       }\n340:     }\n341:     const tally = (kind) => report.reduce((n, r) => n + r.unreachable.filter((u) => u.kind === kind).length, 0);\n342:     const errs = tally('probe-error');\n343:     const wrong = tally('wrong-hub');\n344:     const lost = tally('outranked');\n345:     const none = tally('no-reach');\n346:     const allowed = tally('allowed');\n347:     const local = tally('operator-local');\n348:     process.stdout.write(`\\nchecked=${report.length} hub(s), wrong-hub=${wrong}, outranked=${lost}, no-reach=${none}, allowed=${allowed}, probe-error=${errs}, operator-local=${local}\\n`);\n349:     process.stdout.write(`advisor generation: ${generation == null ? 'unknown' : generation}\\n`);\n350:     // A phrase that reaches nobody is reported rather than failed: it is usually a\n351:     // length limit, and failing on it would make the check unrunnable. Everything\n352:     // else is a real defect, and a probe that never ran is the worst of them,\n353:     // because it is the one that would otherwise print a pass.\n354:     process.stdout.write(errs + wrong + lost ? 'RESULT: FAILED\\n' : 'RESULT: PASSED\\n');\n355:   }\n356:   const failed = report.some((r) => r.unreachable.some((u) => !NON_FAILING.has(u.kind)));\n357:   process.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);\n358: }\n359: \n360: if (require.main === module) main();\n361: \n362: module.exports = { DEFAULT_CONCURRENCY };"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-router-vocabulary-reach.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794526811052,"end":1794526811875}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794526812749,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"id":"prt_1d2221a4d001Y8d8NVM2D7979W","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d221fbe70010zQ6dp594YUUkv","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"8478a401cd348ff1a465414c6ed4fb33572eae2e","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":6017,"output":144,"reasoning":561,"cache":{"read":23424,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794526814835,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"id":"prt_1d222227300163gjC1YD7O0DQH","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d2221a510019r3brcQVVpBblS","type":"step-start","snapshot":"091fbfa8a8dd8b48b49c16dd607144ada6e9dc55"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794526826364,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2224edd001vdNkXKhpGsVnvQ","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d2221a510019r3brcQVVpBblS","type":"tool","id":"call_50fa46591f0740f29378e336","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-code"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-code","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-code\"}"},"time":{"start":1794526826205,"end":1794526826364}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794526826379,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2224b940010pGndFN7wutaTd","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d2221a510019r3brcQVVpBblS","type":"tool","id":"call_e72e601c46ad49fbad27622b","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls -1 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/ 2>/dev/null; echo \"---ROUTER INTENT---\"; rg -n \"INTENT_SIGNALS\" .skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md | head -5; echo \"---ALLOWLIST---\"; cat .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/router-reach-allowlist.json 2>/dev/null | head -40"},"output":"benchmark\nchangelog\ncommand-metadata.json\ndescription.json\nfeature-catalog\ngraph-metadata.json\nhub-router.json\nleaf-aliases.json\nleaf-manifest.json\nleaf-scopes.json\nmanual-testing-playbook\nmode-registry.json\nnode_modules\npackage-lock.json\npackage.json\nREADME.md\nROUTER.md\nscripts\nshared\nsk-create-agent\nsk-create-benchmark\nsk-create-changelog\nsk-create-command\nsk-create-diff\nsk-create-feature-catalog\nsk-create-frontmatter\nsk-create-goal\nsk-create-manual-testing-playbook\nsk-create-quality-control\nsk-create-readme\nsk-create-repo-rule\nsk-create-skill\nsk-create-with-human-voice\nSKILL.md\n---ROUTER INTENT---\n169:INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n439:The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 3 stays the only replay source.\n452:- The machine-readable `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP` block that the deterministic router-replay and benchmarks parse.\n---ALLOWLIST---\n{\n  \"$comment\": [\n    \"Known cross-hub ownership disputes the reach check may pass over.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"Each entry pins the phrase, the hub that declares it, and the hub that currently\",\n    \"wins it. Both sides are required on purpose: an allowlist that only names the\",\n    \"phrase would swallow the next regression on that phrase too, and the whole reason\",\n    \"this check exists is that a broad pass hid 155 broken phrases behind a green run.\",\n    \"If a different hub starts winning an allowed phrase, that is new and it fails.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"Every entry needs a reason and a review date. An entry nobody can justify on\",\n    \"re-read is an entry that should be a fix instead.\"\n  ],\n  \"reviewed\": \"2026-09-07\",\n  \"reviewBy\": \"2026-12-07\",\n  \"entries\": [\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"design review\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"sk-design declares this exactly and still loses to a hub that declares nothing matching. Not resolvable by vocabulary; belongs to the lexical-lane work.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"review this screen\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same shape as 'design review': exact declaration, no matching declaration on the winner.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"design review of this slide deck\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same, and the winning hub has no slide-deck capability at all.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"review this deck\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same shape.\"\n    },\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"benchmark\nchangelog\ncommand-metadata.json\ndescription.json\nfeature-catalog\ngraph-metadata.json\nhub-router.json\nleaf-aliases.json\nleaf-manifest.json\nleaf-scopes.json\nmanual-testing-playbook\nmode-registry.json\nnode_modules\npackage-lock.json\npackage.json\nREADME.md\nROUTER.md\nscripts\nshared\nsk-create-agent\nsk-create-benchmark\nsk-create-changelog\nsk-create-command\nsk-create-diff\nsk-create-feature-catalog\nsk-create-frontmatter\nsk-create-goal\nsk-create-manual-testing-playbook\nsk-create-quality-control\nsk-create-readme\nsk-create-repo-rule\nsk-create-skill\nsk-create-with-human-voice\nSKILL.md\n---ROUTER INTENT---\n169:INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n439:The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 3 stays the only replay source.\n452:- The machine-readable `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP` block that the deterministic router-replay and benchmarks parse.\n---ALLOWLIST---\n{\n  \"$comment\": [\n    \"Known cross-hub ownership disputes the reach check may pass over.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"Each entry pins the phrase, the hub that declares it, and the hub that currently\",\n    \"wins it. Both sides are required on purpose: an allowlist that only names the\",\n    \"phrase would swallow the next regression on that phrase too, and the whole reason\",\n    \"this check exists is that a broad pass hid 155 broken phrases behind a green run.\",\n    \"If a different hub starts winning an allowed phrase, that is new and it fails.\",\n    \"\",\n    \"Every entry needs a reason and a review date. An entry nobody can justify on\",\n    \"re-read is an entry that should be a fix instead.\"\n  ],\n  \"reviewed\": \"2026-09-07\",\n  \"reviewBy\": \"2026-12-07\",\n  \"entries\": [\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"design review\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"sk-design declares this exactly and still loses to a hub that declares nothing matching. Not resolvable by vocabulary; belongs to the lexical-lane work.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"review this screen\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same shape as 'design review': exact declaration, no matching declaration on the winner.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"design review of this slide deck\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same, and the winning hub has no slide-deck capability at all.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"phrase\": \"review this deck\",\n      \"declaredBy\": \"sk-design\",\n      \"wonBy\": \"sk-code\",\n      \"reason\": \"Same shape.\"\n    },\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"ls -1 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/ 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---ROUTER INTENT---\\\"; rg -n \\\"INTENT_SIGNALS\\\" .skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md | head -5; echo \\\"---ALLOWLIST---\\\"; cat .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/router-reach-allowlist.json 2>/dev/null | head -40\"}"},"time":{"start":1794526825364,"end":1794526826379}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794526827406,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"id":"prt_1d222538e00118LGiVz5GuQnKO","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d2221a510019r3brcQVVpBblS","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"ffceed5a15d615687f2f5cd80204d087806cc4e6","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":8603,"output":167,"reasoning":1261,"cache":{"read":29440,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794526829354,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"id":"prt_1d2225b2a0017VYpKTVbZSr9nQ","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d22253930011xk3KTfyC3h3Cv","type":"step-start","snapshot":"a5caba4d7dc35266dc2ba5cdd1b0931f889ae76b"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794526832577,"sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22265c1001Nac2kFtNkXziGf","sessionID":"ses_e2ddecfe0ffezASL7yi66CNWdG","messageID":"msg_1d22253930011xk3KTfyC3h3Cv","type":"tool","id":"call_98df6b77420a4a7e8784f7cc","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md, lines 1-325\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-quality\n3: description: \"sk-code quality gate after implementation, before verification: P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene, and surface checklists.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.13.19.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # Code Quality (quality)\n11: \n12: `quality` is the author-side quality gate MODE child of the `sk-code` family.\n13: It runs after the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` changes the code and before the surface's verification workflow or done-claim.\n14: It consumes the shared surface router, loads the right checklist for the detected surface and target path, fixes quality-gate failures in place, and leaves findings-only output to `sk-code-review`.\n15: \n16: ---\n17: \n18: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n19: \n20: ### Activation Triggers\n21: \n22: Use this mode when the request involves:\n23: - Running the post-implementation quality gate before a completion claim.\n24: - Applying P0/P1/P2 author-side checks to changed code, scripts, skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, or spec docs.\n25: - Checking comment hygiene on modified files and removing ephemeral artifact references from comments while preserving durable WHY.\n26: - Loading a SKILLED authoring checklist for `.skilled/skills/`, `.skilled/agents/`, `.skilled/commands/`, `specs/`, MCP server source, or language-specific SKILLED files.\n27: - Fixing quality-gate failures in place with `Edit` when the fix belongs to the already-authored file.\n28: \n29: Keyword triggers: `quality gate`, `code quality`, `comment hygiene`, `surface checklist`, `authoring checklist`, `SKILLED checklist`, `check before done`.\n30: \n31: ### When NOT to Use\n32: \n33: Skip this mode when:\n34: - The user needs code written, files scaffolded, or behavior implemented. Hand it to the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` for the detected surface.\n35: - A failure needs symptom-to-root-cause investigation or a single-cause bug fix. Use the surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`).\n36: - The task is to collect final non-mutating verification evidence. Use the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`).\n37: - The user asks for findings-first review output, severity-ranked findings, or PR review. Use `sk-code-review`.\n38: - The task is documentation-only prose with no code-work contract. Use `sk-doc`.\n39: \n40: ### Family Boundary\n41: \n42: This is an independently invokable member of the `sk-code` family. It owns the quality gate, not implementation planning, debugging, verification evidence, or formal review reporting. It may edit files that the surface skill already changed to satisfy gate failures, but it does not create new files and does not dispatch subagents.\n43: \n44: Pairs well with:\n45: - The surface skill (`sk-code-skilled`) immediately before this gate, because implementation writes the files this mode checks.\n46: - The surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`) when a quality failure reveals a functional bug or failing command.\n47: - The surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) after P0 quality issues are clear, because verification gates the final claim.\n48: - `sk-code-review` when the user wants findings-only output rather than author-side correction.\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n53: \n54: The `sk-code` hub routes a post-implementation quality gate here: the request names a quality gate, comment hygiene, an authoring checklist or a check before done, and the hub's `quality` signal selects this packet.\n55: This mode routes by target path more than by keyword, because the checklists a change needs follow the files it touches.\n56: `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` names those checklists and why, and its answer outranks any keyword match.\n57: \n58: Three resources load first: `references/runner-contract.md` for the runner's CLI, gate values and verdict;\n59: `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` for the shared severity floor; and `references/code-style-guide.md` for this packet's style and comment rules.\n60: The resource domains, loading levels, path-keyed rows and comment-hygiene layers follow in the next section.\n61: \n62: The prompt-intent projection in that router only makes the default resources scoreable; it never picks the path-keyed checklist.\n63: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: ## 3. RESOURCES\n68: \n69: ### Resource Domains\n70: \n71: - This mode ships no house-style checklist. The universal floor and the universal style guide apply on every surface, and each surface's own checklists carry its house style.\n72:   `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` is the SKILLED baseline, loaded when SKILLED is a resolved surface of the target, and the SKILLED JavaScript and CSS house checks sit under `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/`.\n73: - `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/` contains OpenCode authoring checklists for skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, language files, and config; the spec-folder checklist is owned by `system-spec-kit`.\n74: - `scripts/quality-gate.cjs` is the runner. It reads the change, runs the built-in checks, probes the enforcement layers, lists the checklists the declarations name and prints one verdict. It writes no file.\n75:   The only commands it runs beyond its own are the ones a repository's committed `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` names, each as an argv array under a deadline and the workspace guard, and only once the repository is trusted (`references/quality-config.md` section 8).\n76:   A command a manifest implies is reported as a suggestion it never runs.\n77:   `references/runner-contract.md` holds its CLI, gate values, verdict, envelope grammar and rule ids, and `references/quality-config.md` holds the repository config: its schema, discovery order, merge rule and trust boundary.\n78: - `assets/quality-defaults.json` holds the runner's base layer: excluded paths, sensor settings and the checklist rows this mode owns. It names no command.\n79: - `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` is the per-file comment-hygiene gate. The runner calls it once per run with `--json`.\n80: - `scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh` checks generated/distribution artifact drift when that is part of the target.\n81: - `scripts/ceiling-report.sh [<file>...]` lists the `ceiling:` and `intentional-limit:` comment markers and tags the ones with no trigger or no measurable signal.\n82:   Run it for a debt pass, before a release or when a reviewer asks what limits the code knowingly accepts.\n83:   It reads every tracked code file, or only the files named, prints one `path:line  text  [tags]` line per marker and a closing `markers=N no-trigger=N no-signal=N` line, and exits 0 because it reports and does not gate.\n84: - `scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh` is the legacy write-time hook, kept for direct tests and registered in no runtime. The live write-time warning is `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/claude/claude-posttooluse.cjs`.\n85: - `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` is the cross-mode severity standard this packet consumes, and `references/code-style-guide.md` is this packet's own style and comment-hygiene guide.\n86: \n87: ### Resource Loading Levels\n88: \n89: | Level | When to Load | Resources |\n90: | --- | --- | --- |\n91: | ALWAYS | Any quality-gate invocation | `../shared/references/stack-detection.md`, `../ROUTER.md`, `../shared/references/phase-detection.md` |\n92: | ALWAYS | Before any implementation-done claim | `references/runner-contract.md`, `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`, `references/code-style-guide.md` |\n93: | ALWAYS | Any quality-gate invocation on changed files | `scripts/quality-gate.cjs`, which calls `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` once over every modified file |\n94: | CONDITIONAL | The verdict lists a checklist | Every file in the runner's `checklists[]`, each with the `why` the verdict gives |\n95: | CONDITIONAL | The repository has or needs `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json`, or the verdict reports a configured check, a config error or a suggestion | `references/quality-config.md` |\n96: | CONDITIONAL | SKILLED is a resolved surface of the target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` |\n97: | CONDITIONAL | A direct check on one file, outside a runner call | `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` |\n98: | CONDITIONAL | `.skilled/skills/` target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n99: | CONDITIONAL | `.skilled/agents/` target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n100: | CONDITIONAL | `.skilled/commands/` target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n101: | CONDITIONAL | `specs/` target | `../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` (system-spec-kit) |\n102: | CONDITIONAL | MCP server source | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md` |\n103: | CONDITIONAL | OpenCode JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, JSON, or JSONC files | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` as applicable |\n104: | CONDITIONAL | Generated distribution artifacts or mirrored outputs changed | `scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh` |\n105: | ON_DEMAND | Debt pass, before a release or a reviewer asks what limits the code knowingly accepts | `scripts/ceiling-report.sh` |\n106: | ON_DEMAND | Need the legacy write-time hook's behavior, which no runtime registers | `scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh` |\n107: \n108: ### Target-Path Checklist Map\n109: \n110: The runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows this mode owns in `assets/quality-defaults.json`, which are the universal floor and, in this hub's own repository, the spec-folder authoring checklist, and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration.\n111: Ask the runner which checklists apply to a file, and why, with `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>`. Its `checklists[]` and each entry's `why` are the answer, in any repository.\n112: This repository's own map, the SKILLED rows among them, is rendered in `../sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md` under \"Quality Checklist Map\".\n113: `/doctor:sk-code quality` shows the same answer for a whole repository: every checklist the gate would load, its seat, its override rows and the hygiene rules that apply.\n114: \n115: **A repository outside this hub declares its rules in its own config.** The runner reads surface declarations only from the hub that holds it: the hub's `mode-registry.json` and the `graph-metadata.json` of each skill in the skills folder beside that hub.\n116: - A surface skill that lives in the repository under test, or in any other checkout, is never discovered, even when it declares `surface_detection` and a `quality` block.\n117: - Such a repository names its rules in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` instead.\n118: - Each `checklists[]` row names a repository-relative checklist file and selects files by `paths`, `extensions` or `contentMarkers`.\n119: - A file the row names may carry a `## Rule overrides` section, read through the shared linter `../shared/scripts/check-rule-overrides.cjs`, to drop or replace a rule outside the hard floor at the repository level, which ranks above every surface.\n120: - The limit stays: such a row adds a checklist and never a surface label, so `--explain` lists the checklist while its `surfaces` and `surface_skills` never name a surface of the repository's own.\n121: - `references/quality-config.md` section 3 holds the row rules.\n122: \n123: ### Comment-Hygiene Enforcement Gates\n124: \n125: This mode owns the author-side check and knows the three independent enforcement layers. In any repository, the runner's `layers` field reports what a probe found for each one, `present`, `absent`, `untrusted`, `broken` or `unknown`, and a quality claim cites that field, never this table.\n126: \n127: **This repository's wiring.** The table and the notes after it describe this hub's own repository. They are not universal: another repository may wire none of these layers, or wire them differently.\n128: \n129: | Gate | Where | Effect |\n130: | --- | --- | --- |\n131: | Write-time warning | `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/claude/claude-posttooluse.cjs`, wired as the Claude `PostToolUse` hook for `Write` and `Edit` in `.claude/settings.json` | Warns during authoring when a comment carries ephemeral artifact labels. |\n132: | Pre-commit block | `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit`, installed through `core.hooksPath` by `.skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh` | Blocks commits with forbidden comment patterns across runtimes. |\n133: | CI block | `.github/workflows/comment-hygiene.yml` | Blocks pull requests with forbidden comment patterns. |\n134: \n135: A repository can switch named core hygiene rules off with `commentHygiene.disableRules` in its quality config, never a rule the shared `rule-overrides.json` fixes.\n136: The runner, the pre-commit block and the CI block all read that key through `scripts/lib/quality-config.cjs`, so a rule switched off for one is off for all three.\n137: The config's additions, such as a ticket prefix, reach the runner alone, so the pre-commit and CI blocks keep the core set minus the disabled rules, and `config.hygiene.disabled` in the verdict names what is off.\n138: The write-time warning still applies every core rule, and it never blocks.\n139: `references/quality-config.md` holds the key's rules.\n140: \n141: The write-time warning is one adapter of the shared router in `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/`, which runs after every edit through one adapter per runtime (Claude Code, Codex, Devin, Cursor, Pi and OpenCode) and reports findings before the model's next turn.\n142: The pre-commit block can be bypassed for one commit with `SPECKIT_SKIP_COMMENT_HYGIENE=1 git commit`, and the CI block re-checks every PR to main, which `--no-verify` cannot skip.\n143: The live `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` also runs the agent-mirror-sync gate and other repository gates, independent of comment hygiene, documented in `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/README.md`.\n144: The older `.skilled/hooks/git/pre-commit` and `scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh` are compatibility helpers kept for direct tests. Neither is an installed hook.\n145: \n146: Run `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs`, which calls `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` once over every modified file that can contain comments.\n147: A finding on a changed line blocks.\n148: A finding on an unchanged line of a touched file blocks only when `layers` reports the pre-commit or CI layer `present`, because that layer scans whole files and would fail the change anyway.\n149: Otherwise it is P1 touched-file debt the change did not write.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 4. HOW IT WORKS\n154: \n155: ### Quality Gate Workflow\n156: \n157: 1. Resolve the surface and lifecycle state through the shared router. If no implementation changed files yet, route to the appropriate surface skill (`sk-code-skilled`) unless the user explicitly asked for a standalone quality audit.\n158: 2. Collect the changed-file set from the task context or targeted paths. Read each target before editing.\n159: 3. Run `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs` from the repository root. It takes the working-tree change by default, or `--base <ref>` or `--files <path>...`. Read the gate before anything else.\n160:    `BLOCKED` means fix in place or escalate.\n161:    `UNVERIFIED` means a required check produced no evidence or two override sources at one level disagree on a rule, so hand each check or rule id in `summary.unverified` to verification as an open claim.\n162:    `NOTHING_CHECKED` is reported as such and never as clean. `ERROR` stops the gate until its `errors[]` entry is repaired.\n163: 4. Load every checklist the verdict's `checklists[]` names, with the `why` it gives. The universal floor `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` is always one of them.\n164: 5. Judge each item in `judgment_required`. No built-in check decides input validation, silent failures, initialization safety, commented-out code, accessibility or design restraint.\n165: 6. Apply the P0/P1/P2 model: P0 blocks completion, P1 should be fixed before handoff unless explicitly accepted, P2 can be documented when there is a clear reason.\n166: 7. Before a gate fix adds code, apply the Design Restraint Ladder in `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` and stop at the first rung that holds.\n167: 8. Fix gate failures in place with `Edit` when the correction is limited to already-authored files, then rerun the runner.\n168: 9. If a gate failure requires new files, broader implementation, or behavior design, hand back to the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md`.\n169: 10. If a gate failure is caused by an unclear runtime failure, hand to the surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`) with the observed command, output, and failing target.\n170: 11. When P0 items are clear, render `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --format envelope`, fill its three `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT` fields, and hand to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) for non-mutating evidence before any done-claim.\n171: \n172: ### P0/P1/P2 Author Checks\n173: \n174: The tier rules and the P0 categories live in [`code-quality-standards.md`](../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md): the tiers in its section 2, and the P0 categories with the mechanical floor in its section 3.\n175: \n176: | Severity | Meaning | Gate Effect |\n177: | --- | --- | --- |\n178: | P0 | Correctness, safety, broken contract, or forbidden comment metadata | Blocks completion until fixed or escalated. |\n179: | UNVERIFIED | Unchecked generated drift, missing required checklist evidence, a required check that produced no evidence, or two override sources at one level that disagree on a rule | Neither clean nor blocked. The claim stays open until verification proves it with its own command or reports it open. |\n180: | P1 | Maintainability, routing, style, or authoring-contract issue likely to confuse future work | Fix before handoff unless the user accepts a documented risk. |\n181: | P2 | Local polish, clarity, or minor consistency issue with low behavioral risk | Fix when cheap; otherwise document the deferral and why it is safe. |\n182: \n183: ### Author-Side, Not Review-Side\n184: \n185: Quality mode is allowed to edit because it is part of the implementation lifecycle. It should leave the workspace better than it found it, but only inside the current scope. If the requested output is a review report, use `sk-code-review`; if the requested output is evidence that commands pass, use the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`).\n186: \n187: ### Comment Hygiene\n188: \n189: Comments should explain durable WHY and constraints. They must not embed temporary artifact labels, spec paths, packet labels, ticket labels, or phase bookkeeping. When this mode finds a violation, it edits the comment to preserve the durable reason or removes the comment if it has no durable value.\n190: \n191: ### Advisory Evidence-Handoff Envelope\n192: \n193: When the orchestrator or surface workflow requests structured evidence, append this advisory envelope after the prose handoff. Never place it in a position that reads as a status verdict, and never let it stand in for the handoff to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`).\n194: \n195: ```text\n196: CODE_QUALITY_RESULT v1\n197: schema_version: code-quality/v1\n198: status: advisory\n199: modified_files: <repo-relative paths, or none>\n200: resolved_surface: <declared surface label(s) in detection order | unknown>\n201: checklists_loaded: <checklist paths loaded, or none>\n202: checker_outputs: code-quality-run/v1 gate=<GATE> exit=<n> p0_blocking=<n> unverified=<ids|none> unchecked=<n> layers=pc:<state>,ci:<state>,pe:<state>\n203: p0_p1_p2_decisions: <per-severity dispositions>\n204: accepted_deferrals: <documented P1/P2 deferrals and why safe, or none>\n205: verification_handoff: workflow-verify.md\n206: remaining_accepted_risk: <one-line residual risk, or none>\n207: ```\n208: \n209: `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --format envelope` renders the machine fields, and `references/runner-contract.md` section 4 holds the grammar.\n210: The three judgment fields, `p0_p1_p2_decisions`, `accepted_deferrals` and `remaining_accepted_risk`, print as `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT` until the agent fills them, and a field that still reads it is open work.\n211: `p0_p1_p2_decisions` also lists each `## Rule overrides` row the run applied, as `override <rule id> <none|replace> from <checklist file>`, and each `override-conflict <rule id> <checklist file> <checklist file>` between two checklists at one precedence level, ahead of the marker.\n212: A conflicting rule keeps its default and is unverified.\n213: A refused override section, or one with a row whose id no rule carries, ends the run `CONFIG_INVALID`, exit 3.\n214: A private surface prints by its label only, so the envelope never names a `local-` skill.\n215: \n216: This envelope is advisory and additive only: its `status` is fixed to `advisory` and MUST NOT be `pass`, `success`, or `done`. Its presence NEVER reads as a completion, done, works, or passing claim, and it never replaces the handoff to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) — it reinforces, and does not relax, the §5 NEVER rule that this mode makes no passing claims.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 5. RULES\n221: \n222: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n223: \n224: 1. Read target files before editing them.\n225: 2. Run the runner, `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs`, before any implementation-done or quality-pass claim, and load every checklist its `checklists[]` names. The universal floor is always one of them.\n226: 3. Resolve surface identity through `../shared/references/stack-detection.md`; do not re-author surface detection in this packet.\n227: 4. Load the SKILLED authoring checklist the verdict names before checking `.skilled/` work, and the MCP server checklist by judgment for MCP server source.\n228: 5. Let the runner call `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` over every modified comment-capable file, or run the checker directly on one file outside a runner call.\n229: 6. Fix P0 issues before handing to verification unless the only safe action is escalation.\n230: 7. Keep fixes scoped to quality-gate failures in files already in scope.\n231: 8. Hand failures that need root-cause investigation to the surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`) with the exact symptom and evidence.\n232: 9. Reread the request before the handoff to verification and name any outcome it asks for that no check covers.\n233: \n234: ### ⛔ NEVER\n235: \n236: 1. Never create new files; this mode has no `Write` authority.\n237: 2. Never dispatch subagents; this mode has no `Task` authority.\n238: 3. Never make completion, done, works, or passing claims; hand to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) for evidence.\n239: 4. Never replace a formal findings-first review; route that to `sk-code-review`.\n240: 5. Never paste or fork shared surface-detection, quality, or style references into this packet.\n241: 6. Never broaden scope into cleanup or refactor work unrelated to the gate failure.\n242: 7. Never allow ephemeral artifact labels in code comments; preserve durable WHY only.\n243: 8. Never add a packet-local `graph-metadata.json`.\n244: 9. Never run a command the repository layer does not name. The runner runs its built-in checks and the commands the repository's committed config names, and this mode adds no command of its own.\n245: 10. Never read `NOTHING_CHECKED` or `UNVERIFIED` as clean. Each names evidence that does not exist yet.\n246: \n247: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n248: \n249: 1. A required P0 check is UNVERIFIED and only the operator can name or install its tool.\n250: 2. The correct checklist cannot be identified from the target path.\n251: 3. A P0 issue requires new files, new behavior, or scope beyond an in-place quality fix.\n252: 4. Comment-hygiene enforcement layers disagree on a blocking result.\n253: 5. Generated artifacts appear stale but the regeneration command is unknown or unsafe to run.\n254: \n255: ---\n256: \n257: ## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n258: \n259: - The quality gate ran after implementation and before verification.\n260: - Surface identity came from the shared router, not packet-local detection logic.\n261: - `scripts/quality-gate.cjs` ran on the change, and its gate is quoted as printed, never restated as a pass.\n262: - Every checklist the verdict's `checklists[]` names was loaded before any quality-pass claim, the universal floor among them.\n263: - The comment-hygiene result came from the runner's single checker call, with zero blocking findings or a documented escalation.\n264: - Each id in `summary.unverified` is handed to verification as an open claim.\n265: - P0 issues are fixed in place or escalated with evidence; P1/P2 handling is explicit.\n266: - No new files were authored by this mode.\n267: - Handoff to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) includes the quality evidence and any remaining accepted risk.\n268: \n269: ---\n270: \n271: ## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n272: \n273: - `sk-code` routes quality prompts here through `mode-registry.json` and keeps the hub routing-only.\n274: - The acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` changes files before this gate runs, then applies the surface's `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`, which `sk-code-skilled` bundles as read-only doctrine.\n275: - `sk-code-review` owns findings-first review output and PR-style severity reporting.\n276: - `system-spec-kit` owns spec-folder documentation, validation, and memory workflows when the broader task requires them.\n277: \n278: ---\n279: \n280: ## 8. REFERENCES AND RELATED RESOURCES\n281: \n282: ### Parent And Shared Router\n283: \n284: - [`../SKILL.md`](../SKILL.md) - Routing-only parent hub.\n285: - [`../mode-registry.json`](../mode-registry.json) - Source of truth for mode tool surfaces and packet identity.\n286: - [`../shared/references/stack-detection.md`](../shared/references/stack-detection.md) - Shared surface detection consumed by every mode.\n287: - [`../ROUTER.md`](../ROUTER.md) - Root stage-two intent and resource routing.\n288: - [`../shared/references/phase-detection.md`](../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - Lifecycle transitions around the quality gate.\n289: \n290: ### Quality References And Assets\n291: \n292: - [`references/runner-contract.md`](references/runner-contract.md) - The runner's CLI, gate values, verdict, envelope grammar, rule ids and reader rules.\n293: - [`references/quality-config.md`](references/quality-config.md) - The repository config `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json`: schema, discovery order, merge rule, error codes, trust boundary and known limits.\n294: - [`assets/quality-defaults.json`](assets/quality-defaults.json) - The runner's base layer: excluded paths, sensor settings and the checklist rows this mode owns.\n295: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md) - SKILLED quality baseline, loaded when SKILLED is a resolved surface.\n296: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md) - Skill authoring checklist.\n297: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md) - Agent authoring checklist.\n298: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md) - Command authoring checklist.\n299: - [`spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`](../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md) - Spec folder authoring checklist (owned by system-spec-kit).\n300: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md) - MCP server authoring checklist.\n301: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md) - JavaScript checklist.\n302: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md) - TypeScript checklist.\n303: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md) - Python checklist.\n304: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md) - Shell checklist.\n305: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md) - Rust checklist overview and P0 parity.\n306: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md) - Rust P0 safety and boundary discipline.\n307: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md) - Rust required P1 checks.\n308: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md) - Rust P2 evidence, validation, and resources.\n309: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md) - JSON and JSONC config checklist.\n310: \n311: ### Scripts\n312: \n313: - [`scripts/quality-gate.cjs`](scripts/quality-gate.cjs) - The quality runner, which prints one verdict for a change in any repository.\n314: - [`scripts/tests/`](scripts/tests/) - The runner's suites, one part per behavior: the named fixtures, the surfaces, the outside-skill rows, the layers and generated rows, the dist adapter, the explain output, the config cases and the unit cases. Run one with `node --test`.\n315: - [`scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh`](scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh) - Per-file comment-hygiene checker.\n316: - [`scripts/tests/check-comment-hygiene.test.sh`](scripts/tests/check-comment-hygiene.test.sh) - Comment-hygiene checker tests.\n317: - [`scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh`](scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh) - Distribution drift checker.\n318: - [`scripts/ceiling-report.sh`](scripts/ceiling-report.sh) - Ceiling-marker report. Run it for a debt pass, before a release or when a reviewer asks what limits the code knowingly accepts.\n319: - [`scripts/tests/ceiling-report.test.sh`](scripts/tests/ceiling-report.test.sh) - Ceiling-marker report tests.\n320: - [`scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh`](scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh) - Legacy write-time comment-hygiene hook, kept for direct tests and registered in no runtime.\n321: \n322: ### Universal Standards\n323: \n324: - [`../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`](../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - Shared code quality standards.\n325: - [`references/code-style-guide.md`](references/code-style-guide.md) - This mode's code style and comment hygiene guide.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md, lines 1-325\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-quality\n3: description: \"sk-code quality gate after implementation, before verification: P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene, and surface checklists.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.13.19.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # Code Quality (quality)\n11: \n12: `quality` is the author-side quality gate MODE child of the `sk-code` family.\n13: It runs after the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` changes the code and before the surface's verification workflow or done-claim.\n14: It consumes the shared surface router, loads the right checklist for the detected surface and target path, fixes quality-gate failures in place, and leaves findings-only output to `sk-code-review`.\n15: \n16: ---\n17: \n18: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n19: \n20: ### Activation Triggers\n21: \n22: Use this mode when the request involves:\n23: - Running the post-implementation quality gate before a completion claim.\n24: - Applying P0/P1/P2 author-side checks to changed code, scripts, skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, or spec docs.\n25: - Checking comment hygiene on modified files and removing ephemeral artifact references from comments while preserving durable WHY.\n26: - Loading a SKILLED authoring checklist for `.skilled/skills/`, `.skilled/agents/`, `.skilled/commands/`, `specs/`, MCP server source, or language-specific SKILLED files.\n27: - Fixing quality-gate failures in place with `Edit` when the fix belongs to the already-authored file.\n28: \n29: Keyword triggers: `quality gate`, `code quality`, `comment hygiene`, `surface checklist`, `authoring checklist`, `SKILLED checklist`, `check before done`.\n30: \n31: ### When NOT to Use\n32: \n33: Skip this mode when:\n34: - The user needs code written, files scaffolded, or behavior implemented. Hand it to the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` for the detected surface.\n35: - A failure needs symptom-to-root-cause investigation or a single-cause bug fix. Use the surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`).\n36: - The task is to collect final non-mutating verification evidence. Use the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`).\n37: - The user asks for findings-first review output, severity-ranked findings, or PR review. Use `sk-code-review`.\n38: - The task is documentation-only prose with no code-work contract. Use `sk-doc`.\n39: \n40: ### Family Boundary\n41: \n42: This is an independently invokable member of the `sk-code` family. It owns the quality gate, not implementation planning, debugging, verification evidence, or formal review reporting. It may edit files that the surface skill already changed to satisfy gate failures, but it does not create new files and does not dispatch subagents.\n43: \n44: Pairs well with:\n45: - The surface skill (`sk-code-skilled`) immediately before this gate, because implementation writes the files this mode checks.\n46: - The surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`) when a quality failure reveals a functional bug or failing command.\n47: - The surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) after P0 quality issues are clear, because verification gates the final claim.\n48: - `sk-code-review` when the user wants findings-only output rather than author-side correction.\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n53: \n54: The `sk-code` hub routes a post-implementation quality gate here: the request names a quality gate, comment hygiene, an authoring checklist or a check before done, and the hub's `quality` signal selects this packet.\n55: This mode routes by target path more than by keyword, because the checklists a change needs follow the files it touches.\n56: `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` names those checklists and why, and its answer outranks any keyword match.\n57: \n58: Three resources load first: `references/runner-contract.md` for the runner's CLI, gate values and verdict;\n59: `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` for the shared severity floor; and `references/code-style-guide.md` for this packet's style and comment rules.\n60: The resource domains, loading levels, path-keyed rows and comment-hygiene layers follow in the next section.\n61: \n62: The prompt-intent projection in that router only makes the default resources scoreable; it never picks the path-keyed checklist.\n63: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: ## 3. RESOURCES\n68: \n69: ### Resource Domains\n70: \n71: - This mode ships no house-style checklist. The universal floor and the universal style guide apply on every surface, and each surface's own checklists carry its house style.\n72:   `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` is the SKILLED baseline, loaded when SKILLED is a resolved surface of the target, and the SKILLED JavaScript and CSS house checks sit under `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/`.\n73: - `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/` contains OpenCode authoring checklists for skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, language files, and config; the spec-folder checklist is owned by `system-spec-kit`.\n74: - `scripts/quality-gate.cjs` is the runner. It reads the change, runs the built-in checks, probes the enforcement layers, lists the checklists the declarations name and prints one verdict. It writes no file.\n75:   The only commands it runs beyond its own are the ones a repository's committed `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` names, each as an argv array under a deadline and the workspace guard, and only once the repository is trusted (`references/quality-config.md` section 8).\n76:   A command a manifest implies is reported as a suggestion it never runs.\n77:   `references/runner-contract.md` holds its CLI, gate values, verdict, envelope grammar and rule ids, and `references/quality-config.md` holds the repository config: its schema, discovery order, merge rule and trust boundary.\n78: - `assets/quality-defaults.json` holds the runner's base layer: excluded paths, sensor settings and the checklist rows this mode owns. It names no command.\n79: - `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` is the per-file comment-hygiene gate. The runner calls it once per run with `--json`.\n80: - `scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh` checks generated/distribution artifact drift when that is part of the target.\n81: - `scripts/ceiling-report.sh [<file>...]` lists the `ceiling:` and `intentional-limit:` comment markers and tags the ones with no trigger or no measurable signal.\n82:   Run it for a debt pass, before a release or when a reviewer asks what limits the code knowingly accepts.\n83:   It reads every tracked code file, or only the files named, prints one `path:line  text  [tags]` line per marker and a closing `markers=N no-trigger=N no-signal=N` line, and exits 0 because it reports and does not gate.\n84: - `scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh` is the legacy write-time hook, kept for direct tests and registered in no runtime. The live write-time warning is `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/claude/claude-posttooluse.cjs`.\n85: - `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` is the cross-mode severity standard this packet consumes, and `references/code-style-guide.md` is this packet's own style and comment-hygiene guide.\n86: \n87: ### Resource Loading Levels\n88: \n89: | Level | When to Load | Resources |\n90: | --- | --- | --- |\n91: | ALWAYS | Any quality-gate invocation | `../shared/references/stack-detection.md`, `../ROUTER.md`, `../shared/references/phase-detection.md` |\n92: | ALWAYS | Before any implementation-done claim | `references/runner-contract.md`, `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`, `references/code-style-guide.md` |\n93: | ALWAYS | Any quality-gate invocation on changed files | `scripts/quality-gate.cjs`, which calls `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` once over every modified file |\n94: | CONDITIONAL | The verdict lists a checklist | Every file in the runner's `checklists[]`, each with the `why` the verdict gives |\n95: | CONDITIONAL | The repository has or needs `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json`, or the verdict reports a configured check, a config error or a suggestion | `references/quality-config.md` |\n96: | CONDITIONAL | SKILLED is a resolved surface of the target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` |\n97: | CONDITIONAL | A direct check on one file, outside a runner call | `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` |\n98: | CONDITIONAL | `.skilled/skills/` target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n99: | CONDITIONAL | `.skilled/agents/` target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n100: | CONDITIONAL | `.skilled/commands/` target | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n101: | CONDITIONAL | `specs/` target | `../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` (system-spec-kit) |\n102: | CONDITIONAL | MCP server source | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md` |\n103: | CONDITIONAL | OpenCode JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, JSON, or JSONC files | `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`, `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` as applicable |\n104: | CONDITIONAL | Generated distribution artifacts or mirrored outputs changed | `scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh` |\n105: | ON_DEMAND | Debt pass, before a release or a reviewer asks what limits the code knowingly accepts | `scripts/ceiling-report.sh` |\n106: | ON_DEMAND | Need the legacy write-time hook's behavior, which no runtime registers | `scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh` |\n107: \n108: ### Target-Path Checklist Map\n109: \n110: The runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows this mode owns in `assets/quality-defaults.json`, which are the universal floor and, in this hub's own repository, the spec-folder authoring checklist, and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration.\n111: Ask the runner which checklists apply to a file, and why, with `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>`. Its `checklists[]` and each entry's `why` are the answer, in any repository.\n112: This repository's own map, the SKILLED rows among them, is rendered in `../sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md` under \"Quality Checklist Map\".\n113: `/doctor:sk-code quality` shows the same answer for a whole repository: every checklist the gate would load, its seat, its override rows and the hygiene rules that apply.\n114: \n115: **A repository outside this hub declares its rules in its own config.** The runner reads surface declarations only from the hub that holds it: the hub's `mode-registry.json` and the `graph-metadata.json` of each skill in the skills folder beside that hub.\n116: - A surface skill that lives in the repository under test, or in any other checkout, is never discovered, even when it declares `surface_detection` and a `quality` block.\n117: - Such a repository names its rules in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` instead.\n118: - Each `checklists[]` row names a repository-relative checklist file and selects files by `paths`, `extensions` or `contentMarkers`.\n119: - A file the row names may carry a `## Rule overrides` section, read through the shared linter `../shared/scripts/check-rule-overrides.cjs`, to drop or replace a rule outside the hard floor at the repository level, which ranks above every surface.\n120: - The limit stays: such a row adds a checklist and never a surface label, so `--explain` lists the checklist while its `surfaces` and `surface_skills` never name a surface of the repository's own.\n121: - `references/quality-config.md` section 3 holds the row rules.\n122: \n123: ### Comment-Hygiene Enforcement Gates\n124: \n125: This mode owns the author-side check and knows the three independent enforcement layers. In any repository, the runner's `layers` field reports what a probe found for each one, `present`, `absent`, `untrusted`, `broken` or `unknown`, and a quality claim cites that field, never this table.\n126: \n127: **This repository's wiring.** The table and the notes after it describe this hub's own repository. They are not universal: another repository may wire none of these layers, or wire them differently.\n128: \n129: | Gate | Where | Effect |\n130: | --- | --- | --- |\n131: | Write-time warning | `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/claude/claude-posttooluse.cjs`, wired as the Claude `PostToolUse` hook for `Write` and `Edit` in `.claude/settings.json` | Warns during authoring when a comment carries ephemeral artifact labels. |\n132: | Pre-commit block | `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit`, installed through `core.hooksPath` by `.skilled/scripts/install-git-hooks.sh` | Blocks commits with forbidden comment patterns across runtimes. |\n133: | CI block | `.github/workflows/comment-hygiene.yml` | Blocks pull requests with forbidden comment patterns. |\n134: \n135: A repository can switch named core hygiene rules off with `commentHygiene.disableRules` in its quality config, never a rule the shared `rule-overrides.json` fixes.\n136: The runner, the pre-commit block and the CI block all read that key through `scripts/lib/quality-config.cjs`, so a rule switched off for one is off for all three.\n137: The config's additions, such as a ticket prefix, reach the runner alone, so the pre-commit and CI blocks keep the core set minus the disabled rules, and `config.hygiene.disabled` in the verdict names what is off.\n138: The write-time warning still applies every core rule, and it never blocks.\n139: `references/quality-config.md` holds the key's rules.\n140: \n141: The write-time warning is one adapter of the shared router in `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/`, which runs after every edit through one adapter per runtime (Claude Code, Codex, Devin, Cursor, Pi and OpenCode) and reports findings before the model's next turn.\n142: The pre-commit block can be bypassed for one commit with `SPECKIT_SKIP_COMMENT_HYGIENE=1 git commit`, and the CI block re-checks every PR to main, which `--no-verify` cannot skip.\n143: The live `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` also runs the agent-mirror-sync gate and other repository gates, independent of comment hygiene, documented in `.skilled/scripts/git-hooks/README.md`.\n144: The older `.skilled/hooks/git/pre-commit` and `scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh` are compatibility helpers kept for direct tests. Neither is an installed hook.\n145: \n146: Run `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs`, which calls `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` once over every modified file that can contain comments.\n147: A finding on a changed line blocks.\n148: A finding on an unchanged line of a touched file blocks only when `layers` reports the pre-commit or CI layer `present`, because that layer scans whole files and would fail the change anyway.\n149: Otherwise it is P1 touched-file debt the change did not write.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 4. HOW IT WORKS\n154: \n155: ### Quality Gate Workflow\n156: \n157: 1. Resolve the surface and lifecycle state through the shared router. If no implementation changed files yet, route to the appropriate surface skill (`sk-code-skilled`) unless the user explicitly asked for a standalone quality audit.\n158: 2. Collect the changed-file set from the task context or targeted paths. Read each target before editing.\n159: 3. Run `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs` from the repository root. It takes the working-tree change by default, or `--base <ref>` or `--files <path>...`. Read the gate before anything else.\n160:    `BLOCKED` means fix in place or escalate.\n161:    `UNVERIFIED` means a required check produced no evidence or two override sources at one level disagree on a rule, so hand each check or rule id in `summary.unverified` to verification as an open claim.\n162:    `NOTHING_CHECKED` is reported as such and never as clean. `ERROR` stops the gate until its `errors[]` entry is repaired.\n163: 4. Load every checklist the verdict's `checklists[]` names, with the `why` it gives. The universal floor `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` is always one of them.\n164: 5. Judge each item in `judgment_required`. No built-in check decides input validation, silent failures, initialization safety, commented-out code, accessibility or design restraint.\n165: 6. Apply the P0/P1/P2 model: P0 blocks completion, P1 should be fixed before handoff unless explicitly accepted, P2 can be documented when there is a clear reason.\n166: 7. Before a gate fix adds code, apply the Design Restraint Ladder in `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` and stop at the first rung that holds.\n167: 8. Fix gate failures in place with `Edit` when the correction is limited to already-authored files, then rerun the runner.\n168: 9. If a gate failure requires new files, broader implementation, or behavior design, hand back to the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md`.\n169: 10. If a gate failure is caused by an unclear runtime failure, hand to the surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`) with the observed command, output, and failing target.\n170: 11. When P0 items are clear, render `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --format envelope`, fill its three `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT` fields, and hand to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) for non-mutating evidence before any done-claim.\n171: \n172: ### P0/P1/P2 Author Checks\n173: \n174: The tier rules and the P0 categories live in [`code-quality-standards.md`](../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md): the tiers in its section 2, and the P0 categories with the mechanical floor in its section 3.\n175: \n176: | Severity | Meaning | Gate Effect |\n177: | --- | --- | --- |\n178: | P0 | Correctness, safety, broken contract, or forbidden comment metadata | Blocks completion until fixed or escalated. |\n179: | UNVERIFIED | Unchecked generated drift, missing required checklist evidence, a required check that produced no evidence, or two override sources at one level that disagree on a rule | Neither clean nor blocked. The claim stays open until verification proves it with its own command or reports it open. |\n180: | P1 | Maintainability, routing, style, or authoring-contract issue likely to confuse future work | Fix before handoff unless the user accepts a documented risk. |\n181: | P2 | Local polish, clarity, or minor consistency issue with low behavioral risk | Fix when cheap; otherwise document the deferral and why it is safe. |\n182: \n183: ### Author-Side, Not Review-Side\n184: \n185: Quality mode is allowed to edit because it is part of the implementation lifecycle. It should leave the workspace better than it found it, but only inside the current scope. If the requested output is a review report, use `sk-code-review`; if the requested output is evidence that commands pass, use the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`).\n186: \n187: ### Comment Hygiene\n188: \n189: Comments should explain durable WHY and constraints. They must not embed temporary artifact labels, spec paths, packet labels, ticket labels, or phase bookkeeping. When this mode finds a violation, it edits the comment to preserve the durable reason or removes the comment if it has no durable value.\n190: \n191: ### Advisory Evidence-Handoff Envelope\n192: \n193: When the orchestrator or surface workflow requests structured evidence, append this advisory envelope after the prose handoff. Never place it in a position that reads as a status verdict, and never let it stand in for the handoff to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`).\n194: \n195: ```text\n196: CODE_QUALITY_RESULT v1\n197: schema_version: code-quality/v1\n198: status: advisory\n199: modified_files: <repo-relative paths, or none>\n200: resolved_surface: <declared surface label(s) in detection order | unknown>\n201: checklists_loaded: <checklist paths loaded, or none>\n202: checker_outputs: code-quality-run/v1 gate=<GATE> exit=<n> p0_blocking=<n> unverified=<ids|none> unchecked=<n> layers=pc:<state>,ci:<state>,pe:<state>\n203: p0_p1_p2_decisions: <per-severity dispositions>\n204: accepted_deferrals: <documented P1/P2 deferrals and why safe, or none>\n205: verification_handoff: workflow-verify.md\n206: remaining_accepted_risk: <one-line residual risk, or none>\n207: ```\n208: \n209: `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --format envelope` renders the machine fields, and `references/runner-contract.md` section 4 holds the grammar.\n210: The three judgment fields, `p0_p1_p2_decisions`, `accepted_deferrals` and `remaining_accepted_risk`, print as `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT` until the agent fills them, and a field that still reads it is open work.\n211: `p0_p1_p2_decisions` also lists each `## Rule overrides` row the run applied, as `override <rule id> <none|replace> from <checklist file>`, and each `override-conflict <rule id> <checklist file> <checklist file>` between two checklists at one precedence level, ahead of the marker.\n212: A conflicting rule keeps its default and is unverified.\n213: A refused override section, or one with a row whose id no rule carries, ends the run `CONFIG_INVALID`, exit 3.\n214: A private surface prints by its label only, so the envelope never names a `local-` skill.\n215: \n216: This envelope is advisory and additive only: its `status` is fixed to `advisory` and MUST NOT be `pass`, `success`, or `done`. Its presence NEVER reads as a completion, done, works, or passing claim, and it never replaces the handoff to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) — it reinforces, and does not relax, the §5 NEVER rule that this mode makes no passing claims.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 5. RULES\n221: \n222: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n223: \n224: 1. Read target files before editing them.\n225: 2. Run the runner, `node scripts/quality-gate.cjs`, before any implementation-done or quality-pass claim, and load every checklist its `checklists[]` names. The universal floor is always one of them.\n226: 3. Resolve surface identity through `../shared/references/stack-detection.md`; do not re-author surface detection in this packet.\n227: 4. Load the SKILLED authoring checklist the verdict names before checking `.skilled/` work, and the MCP server checklist by judgment for MCP server source.\n228: 5. Let the runner call `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` over every modified comment-capable file, or run the checker directly on one file outside a runner call.\n229: 6. Fix P0 issues before handing to verification unless the only safe action is escalation.\n230: 7. Keep fixes scoped to quality-gate failures in files already in scope.\n231: 8. Hand failures that need root-cause investigation to the surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`) with the exact symptom and evidence.\n232: 9. Reread the request before the handoff to verification and name any outcome it asks for that no check covers.\n233: \n234: ### ⛔ NEVER\n235: \n236: 1. Never create new files; this mode has no `Write` authority.\n237: 2. Never dispatch subagents; this mode has no `Task` authority.\n238: 3. Never make completion, done, works, or passing claims; hand to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) for evidence.\n239: 4. Never replace a formal findings-first review; route that to `sk-code-review`.\n240: 5. Never paste or fork shared surface-detection, quality, or style references into this packet.\n241: 6. Never broaden scope into cleanup or refactor work unrelated to the gate failure.\n242: 7. Never allow ephemeral artifact labels in code comments; preserve durable WHY only.\n243: 8. Never add a packet-local `graph-metadata.json`.\n244: 9. Never run a command the repository layer does not name. The runner runs its built-in checks and the commands the repository's committed config names, and this mode adds no command of its own.\n245: 10. Never read `NOTHING_CHECKED` or `UNVERIFIED` as clean. Each names evidence that does not exist yet.\n246: \n247: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n248: \n249: 1. A required P0 check is UNVERIFIED and only the operator can name or install its tool.\n250: 2. The correct checklist cannot be identified from the target path.\n251: 3. A P0 issue requires new files, new behavior, or scope beyond an in-place quality fix.\n252: 4. Comment-hygiene enforcement layers disagree on a blocking result.\n253: 5. Generated artifacts appear stale but the regeneration command is unknown or unsafe to run.\n254: \n255: ---\n256: \n257: ## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n258: \n259: - The quality gate ran after implementation and before verification.\n260: - Surface identity came from the shared router, not packet-local detection logic.\n261: - `scripts/quality-gate.cjs` ran on the change, and its gate is quoted as printed, never restated as a pass.\n262: - Every checklist the verdict's `checklists[]` names was loaded before any quality-pass claim, the universal floor among them.\n263: - The comment-hygiene result came from the runner's single checker call, with zero blocking findings or a documented escalation.\n264: - Each id in `summary.unverified` is handed to verification as an open claim.\n265: - P0 issues are fixed in place or escalated with evidence; P1/P2 handling is explicit.\n266: - No new files were authored by this mode.\n267: - Handoff to the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) includes the quality evidence and any remaining accepted risk.\n268: \n269: ---\n270: \n271: ## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n272: \n273: - `sk-code` routes quality prompts here through `mode-registry.json` and keeps the hub routing-only.\n274: - The acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` changes files before this gate runs, then applies the surface's `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`, which `sk-code-skilled` bundles as read-only doctrine.\n275: - `sk-code-review` owns findings-first review output and PR-style severity reporting.\n276: - `system-spec-kit` owns spec-folder documentation, validation, and memory workflows when the broader task requires them.\n277: \n278: ---\n279: \n280: ## 8. REFERENCES AND RELATED RESOURCES\n281: \n282: ### Parent And Shared Router\n283: \n284: - [`../SKILL.md`](../SKILL.md) - Routing-only parent hub.\n285: - [`../mode-registry.json`](../mode-registry.json) - Source of truth for mode tool surfaces and packet identity.\n286: - [`../shared/references/stack-detection.md`](../shared/references/stack-detection.md) - Shared surface detection consumed by every mode.\n287: - [`../ROUTER.md`](../ROUTER.md) - Root stage-two intent and resource routing.\n288: - [`../shared/references/phase-detection.md`](../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - Lifecycle transitions around the quality gate.\n289: \n290: ### Quality References And Assets\n291: \n292: - [`references/runner-contract.md`](references/runner-contract.md) - The runner's CLI, gate values, verdict, envelope grammar, rule ids and reader rules.\n293: - [`references/quality-config.md`](references/quality-config.md) - The repository config `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json`: schema, discovery order, merge rule, error codes, trust boundary and known limits.\n294: - [`assets/quality-defaults.json`](assets/quality-defaults.json) - The runner's base layer: excluded paths, sensor settings and the checklist rows this mode owns.\n295: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md) - SKILLED quality baseline, loaded when SKILLED is a resolved surface.\n296: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md) - Skill authoring checklist.\n297: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md) - Agent authoring checklist.\n298: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md) - Command authoring checklist.\n299: - [`spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`](../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md) - Spec folder authoring checklist (owned by system-spec-kit).\n300: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md) - MCP server authoring checklist.\n301: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md) - JavaScript checklist.\n302: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md) - TypeScript checklist.\n303: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md) - Python checklist.\n304: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md) - Shell checklist.\n305: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md) - Rust checklist overview and P0 parity.\n306: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md) - Rust P0 safety and boundary discipline.\n307: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md) - Rust required P1 checks.\n308: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md) - Rust P2 evidence, validation, and resources.\n309: - [`../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`](../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md) - JSON and JSONC config checklist.\n310: \n311: ### Scripts\n312: \n313: - [`scripts/quality-gate.cjs`](scripts/quality-gate.cjs) - The quality runner, which prints one verdict for a change in any repository.\n314: - [`scripts/tests/`](scripts/tests/) - The runner's suites, one part per behavior: the named fixtures, the surfaces, the outside-skill rows, the layers and generated rows, the dist adapter, the explain output, the config cases and the unit cases. Run one with `node --test`.\n315: - [`scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh`](scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh) - Per-file comment-hygiene checker.\n316: - [`scripts/tests/check-comment-hygiene.test.sh`](scripts/tests/check-comment-hygiene.test.sh) - Comment-hygiene checker tests.\n317: - [`scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh`](scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh) - Distribution drift checker.\n318: - [`scripts/ceiling-report.sh`](scripts/ceiling-report.sh) - Ceiling-marker report. Run it for a debt pass, before a release or when a reviewer asks what limits the code knowingly accepts.\n319: - [`scripts/tests/ceiling-report.test.sh`](scripts/tests/ceiling-report.test.sh) - Ceiling-marker report tests.\n320: - [`scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh`](scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh) - Legacy write-time comment-hygiene hook, kept for direct tests and registered in no runtime.\n321: \n322: ### Universal Standards\n323: \n324: - [`../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`](../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - Shared code quality standards.\n325: - [`references/code-style-guide.md`](references/code-style-guide.md) - This mode's code style and comment hygiene guide."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794526832065,"end":1794526832577}}}}
